# Plans for the new school: I am going to, I want to join, and how I feel about it.
lesson language-next-year v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=9 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Next year"
  goal "Say what you are going to do at your new school next year in [[language.name]], and which club you want to join."

  look {
    scene 24x14 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        comic plans who=["boy", "girl"] says=["[[future.next-year-i-am-going-to-go-to-secondary]]", "[[future.i-am-going-to-learn-french]]"] moods=["excited", "happy"] with=["", ""] thinks=[0, 0] holds=["", ""] labels=["1", "2"]
      }
    }
    say """
      To talk about plans, say [[future.i-am-going-to]] and what you will do:
      [[future.i-am-going-to-learn-french]], I am going to learn French. Next year is
      [[future.next-year]]. A wish is said with [[future.i-want]]:
      [[future.i-want-to-join-the-drama-club]], I want to join the drama club.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.next-year-name k=4
    }
    show language.next-year-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.next-year-meaning k=4 easy-k=1
    show language.next-year-write k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.next-year-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.next-year-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.next-year-write k=4 easy-k=3 hard-k=5
    show language.next-year-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.next-year-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.next-year-write k=3
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.next-year-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.next-year-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[future.next-year-i-am-going-to-go-to-secondary]] Next year I am going to go to secondary school. [[future.i-want-to-join-the-drama-club]]"
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is a plan and a wish taken for each other, and two plans that differ
    by one word: each naming question offers the sentence with one part changed. The new school is
    called [[future.the-secondary-school]] in Spain, where secondary school starts at twelve
    (recalled), and [[future.i-am-going-to]] with a verb is how [[language.name]] talks about a
    plan, much as English says going to. The step up from about me, earlier this grade, which was in
    the present, is plans and wishes for the year ahead, written by the child and read by a grown-up
    with the notice list. From this grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word
    typed without its accent is not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as
    Read aloud, or played by the device. If the child is stuck, talk about the new school in English
    first, then say each plan in the language. Off the page, write a letter to yourself in the
    language to open at the end of next year. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme
    (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "write phrases from memory, and adapt these to create new
    sentences, to express ideas clearly"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Can talk about
    themselves after organizing the contents they want to communicate by using simple words and
    phrases and basic expressions" (Japan's grade six book We Can! 2 has a unit on junior high
    school life, recalled); and CEFR A2, "Can communicate in simple and routine tasks requiring a
    simple and direct exchange of information on familiar and routine matters". The phrases are in
    [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[future.i-want-to-join-the-drama-club]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[future.next-year-i-am-going-to-go-to-secondary]] and [[future.i-am-going-to-learn-french]] and the words to write are [[future.to-study]] and [[future.new]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[future.i-want-to-join-the-chess-club]] and [[future.i-am-going-to-study-hard]] and the words to write are [[future.uniform]], [[future.french]] and [[future.new]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
